[10.230081, "o", "20:example, to make a fix for 4.11.1, base your branch on *4.11* (not on *4.11.1*).\r\n21:The `configure` step for the compiler recognises a development build from the\r\n24:(ocamltest and converting C compiler warnings to errors).\r\n26:2. Consult link:INSTALL.adoc[] for build instructions. Here is the gist of it:\r\n30:make -j 4\r\n32:If you are on a release build and need development options, you can add\r\n33:`--enable-ocamltest` (to allow running the testsuite) and `--enable-warn-error`\r\n40:make runtop\r\n43:4. Hack frenetically and keep rebuilding.\r\n45:5. Run the testsuite from time to time.\r\n48:make tests\r\n54:<<opam-switch,create an opam switch>> to test your modified compiler.\r\n141:and `runtime_NATIVE_ONLY_C_SOURCES` in link:Makefile[] for the list of common,\r\n154:Instructions for building the full reference manual are provided in\r\n160:make html_doc\r\n163:and then opening link:./api_docgen/ocamldoc/build/html/libref/index.html[] in a web browser.\r\n165:link:./api_docgen/odoc/build/html/libref/index.html[] when `--with-odoc` is\r\n181:  BOOTSTRAP.adoc::        instructions for bootstrapping\r\n187:  Makefile::              main Makefile\r\n188:  Makefile.common::       common Makefile definitions\r\n193:  boot/::                 bootstrap compiler\r\n194:  build-aux/::            autotools support scripts\r\n207:  ocamltest/::            test driver\r\n213:  testsuite/::            tests -- see link:testsuite/HACKING.adoc[]\r\n251:=== Testing with `opam`\r\n261:If you want to test someone else's development version from a public\r\n262:git repository, you can build a switch directly (without cloning their\r\n270:==== Incremental builds with `opam`\r\n273:alternate between testing your branch and patching the compiler.\r\n281:`--inplace-build` flag:\r\n285:opam install . --inplace-build\r\n289:manually, as long as you make sure that the configuration prefix is the one\r\n292:(that you must build yourself) or using the regular sandboxed builds.\r\n295:# Example with regular opam build\r\n305:make -j\r\n329:In our case, we need to build the compiler, and when we've built everything\r\n330:that we need then we run `opam custom-install ocaml-variants -- make install`.\r\n331:This will make `opam` remove the previously installed version of the compiler\r\n335:# reinstall the compiler, and rebuild all opam packages\r\n336:opam custom-install ocaml-variants -- make install\r\n350:opam custom-install --no-recompilations ocaml-variants -- make install\r\n369:./configure --prefix=$(opam var prefix) --disable-ocamldoc --disable-ocamltest\r\n370:make world && make opt\r\n371:opam custom-install ocaml-variants -- make install\r\n377:One advantage of this plugin over a plain `make install` is that it\r\n379:`make install` command only installs the bytecode versions of the tools,\r\n381:bytecode tools are installed, whereas with a raw `make install` you will have\r\n383:Since it's significantly faster to build the bytecode version of the tools,\r\n385:present and the bytecode version otherwise, you can build your initial switch\r\n387:then clean your working directory and start building bytecode tools only\r\n396:(`make install`) in the working directory, tracking the installed files\r\n401:You can also run `make install` manually, which will not trigger a\r\n405:=== Useful Makefile targets and options\r\n407:Besides the targets listed in link:INSTALL.adoc[] for build and\r\n410:`make runtop` :: builds and runs the ocaml toplevel of the distribution\r\n412:                          (use `make runtop-with-otherlibs` if you need `Unix` or other\r\n414:`make natruntop`:: builds and runs the native ocaml toplevel (experimental)\r\n416:`make partialclean`:: Clean the OCaml files but keep the compiled C files.\r\n418:`make depend`:: Regenerate the `.depend` file. Should be used each time new dependencies are added between files.\r\n420:`make -C testsuite parallel`:: see link:testsuite/HACKING.adoc[]\r\n422:You can use `make foo V=1` to build the target foo and show full\r\n424:useful to know the flags to use to manually rebuild a file.\r\n426:Additionally, there are some developer specific targets in link:Makefile.dev[].\r\n434:them. For exam"]
[10.231053, "o", "ple, you may wish to avoid building the debug runtime by default\r\n441:override them, for example `./configure --enable-debug-runtime` will build the\r\n448:`configure` includes the standard `-C` option which caches various test results\r\n449:in the file `config.cache` and can use those results to avoid running tests in\r\n463:=== Bootstrapping\r\n465:The OCaml compiler is bootstrapped. This means that\r\n470:standard library and then to build a fresh compiler. Details can be\r\n471:found in link:BOOTSTRAP.adoc[].\r\n473:=== Speeding up builds\r\n476:speed up future builds by copying it to `boot`:\r\n482:If `boot/ocamlc` changes (e.g. because you ran `make bootstrap`), then\r\n483:the build will revert to the slower bytecode-compiled `ocamlc` until\r\n494:To use merlin on the compiler, you want to build the compiler with an older\r\n495:version of itself. One easy way to do this is to use the experimental build\r\n497:the build will work all the time). Assuming you already have a recent OCaml\r\n503:make clean && dune build @libs\r\n506:which will do a bytecode build of all the distribution (without linking\r\n509:Merlin will be looking at the artefacts generated by dune (in `_build`), rather\r\n510:than trying to open the incompatible artefacts produced by a Makefile build. In\r\n511:particular, you need to repeat the dune build every time you change the interface\r\n514:You only need to run `configure` once, but you will need to run `make clean`\r\n515:every time you want to run `dune` after you built something with `make`;\r\n516:otherwise dune will complain that build artefacts are present among the sources.\r\n521:of the compiler requires the `stdlib` library to build, nothing can be build. In\r\n526:version. But it also makes it a lot easier to test the impact of your work on\r\n555:your choice. We use an explicit \"linking exception\" precisely to make\r\n602:then the generated `configure` script might differ too. The latest\r\n610:docker build -t ocaml-make-configure:latest - <<'EOF'\r\n612:FROM ubuntu:latest\r\n616:    apt update && apt-get --no-install-recommends install -y autoconf make\r\n620:git rebase trunk --exec \"docker run --rm -it -v $PWD:/mnt ocaml-make-configure:latest\"\r\n626:link:.github/workflows/build.yml[].\r\n628:For example, if you want to reproduce the default build on your\r\n637:tests which inspect the patch submitted as part of a pull request. These\r\n638:tests rely on ancillary data generated by GitHub Actions which you have to\r\n647:The Windows ports take a long time to test - INRIA's precheck service is the\r\n648:best to use when all 6 Windows ports need testing for a branch, but the\r\n654:- `BOOTSTRAP_FLEXDLL` - must be set on each job. Either `true` or `false`.\r\n655:  At present, must be `false` for Cygwin builds. Controls whether flexlink\r\n656:  is bootstrapped as part of the test or installed from a binary archive.\r\n658:  Cygwin packages as part of the build. Normally a full upgrade is only\r\n660:- `BUILD_MODE`. Default: `world.opt`. Either `world.opt`, `steps`, or `C`.\r\n661:  Controls whether the build uses the `world.opt` target or the classic\r\n662:  `world`, `opt`, `opt.opt` targets. The `C` build is a fast test used to\r\n663:  build just enough of the tree to cover the C sources (it's used to test\r\n666:  to set-up the Microsoft build environment.\r\n668:  Cygwin as the build environment.\r\n672:We use GitHub Actions workflows to test the OCaml compiler and runtime\r\n673:in various settings. Some tests are not run by default and are\r\n676:- `CI: Full matrix`: expands the test matrix considerably with additional\r\n679:- `CI: Skip testsuite`: turns the tesuite *off* for a PR, which can be useful\r\n682:- `run-crosscompiler-tests`: build various cross-compilers and test them;\r\n683:- `run-thread-sanitizer`: build the distribution with the Thread\r\n684:  Sanitizer (TSAN), and run the whole testsuite instrumented with TSAN;\r\n685:- `run-multicoretests`: run an extensive testsuite convering multicore\r\n693:various MacOS versions) than the Travis/AppVeyor testing on github,\r\n698:jenkins service; anyone can create an account there to access build\r\n"]
